nato foreign affairs ministers are  meeting for a special summit on ukraine  it comes as allies tightened sanctions  on vladimir putin's inner circle at a  news conference with the u.s secretary  of state nato's secretary-general  condemned russian shelling on the  zaparica nuclear site as reckless  we condemn the attacks on  civilians and overnight we've also seen  reports about  the attack against nuclear power  this just demonstrates the  recklessness of this war and the  importance of uh  ending it and the importance of russia  withdrawing all its troops  and engaging good faith in diplomatic  efforts  every  ally  in one way or another is coming to  ukraine's assistance every ally in one  way or another is helping uh to  strengthen nato itself  and as the secretary general said ours  is a defensive alliance  we seek no conflict but if conflict  comes to us  we're ready for it and we will defend  every inch of nato territory  our diplomatic editor james baze is live  for us in brussels uh james another  meeting a flurry of meetings in the past  week what's happening today  well yes we've got nato  ministers meeting foreign ministers  they're joined by some other countries  sweden and finland which aren't nato  members  and the european union represented at  this meeting and then the other way  around in the afternoon when it will be  the european union meeting and  non-members of the european union like  the us and the uk  and the nato secretary general will be  meeting attending that meeting i think  the idea is to look at what they've done  so far and see if there's anything more  that they can do  clearly they've gone further i think  than many of the ministers thought they  would at the start of this conflict in  terms of sanctions  and i think they'll be looking at what  other measures they can take but you're  not going to see  these foreign ministers here at nato  doing what the ukrainian government  would want and that's to actively  support um  the ukrainians perhaps with a no-fly  zone a no-fly zone would need to be  enforced by nato aircraft we've seen how  that works in libya we saw how it worked  with kosovo um they will they would have  to be those nato aircraft potentially  shooting down russian aircraft then you  have a war involving nato and russia and  of course the us is a is a member the  biggest member of nato you then  effectively have a world war in world  war iii so that is something that they  will not countenance and i don't think  that is being suggested by any of the 30  nato members who are now meeting in  their closed meeting i think some though  of the nato members and particularly  those that are close to russia have  borders with russia are concerned we've  seen major reinforcement of those  countries in the last couple of weeks uh  but some for example the latvian foreign  minister on his way into the meeting  said it was time to go  from a deterrence mission to a  full-scale defensive mission now what he  means by that whether he wants even more  troops that's not clear but i suspect um  that many of the baltic states would  certainly like to see  that the presence of these troops  including us troops made more permanent  the the nato secretary general has  called the situation now the new normal  and in many ways uh you could divide  nato's history 1945 to 1989 was the cold  war 1989 until a couple of weeks ago was  phase two he now says we're in a new  normal which  some nato nations say will require  different position of of forces in the  future  james bay's diplomatic editor in  brussels thank you
 

